# fixedbugs, defect 507, lane b15-emit, 2026-10-08 (panel 199's completeness
# critic, its first pass, finding 2).
# Symptom: a correct function calling itself on every path but one, the one
# ending in `exit(code:)`, built at exit 0 printing clang's *warning: all
# paths through this function will call itself [-Winfinite-recursion]* over
# the generated C, and ran as written.
# Cause: Heroes ends a path at `exit` (`check/path_end.calls_exit`), and the
# C a call of the library's `exit` became was a call followed by a jump: clang
# read the jump as taken. The call is now followed by `hero_unreachable()`,
# which says in C what Heroes says.
#
# The shapes beside it, each a function of its own: `exit` in an arm with no
# `else`, in an arm with one, and in a loop. One of them runs, and every one
# is built at both levels, which is where the warning was printed. `exit`
# inside a function the self-calling one calls is not here: Heroes does not
# end a path at such a call, so neither can its C, and that shape is
# reported apart.

function next(n: i64) -> i64
    return n + 1

function in_an_arm(n: i64) -> i64
    if n > 3
        exit(code: 0)
    print(n)
    return in_an_arm(next(n))

function in_an_arm_with_an_else(n: i64) -> i64
    if n > 3
        exit(code: 0)
    else
        print(n)
    return in_an_arm_with_an_else(next(n))

function in_a_loop(n: i64) -> i64
    i: i64 @ n

    while i < 10
        if i > 3
            exit(code: 0)
        print(i)
        i @ next(i)

    return in_a_loop(i)

function main()
    if args().len() > 99
        _ = in_an_arm_with_an_else(1)
        _ = in_a_loop(1)
    _ = in_an_arm(1)
